    {
      "chunkId": "vm30-section-1-a-general-2-annual-statement-applicability",
      "sectionReference": "2. Annual statement applicability and exclusions",
      "pageStart": 325,
      "pageEnd": 325,
      "provisionTypes": [
        "scope_or_applicability",
        "actuarial_opinion_requirement",
        "actuarial_memorandum_requirement",
        "documentation_requirement",
        "submission_or_timing_requirement",
        "exception_or_exemption",
        "guidance_note_present",
        "supporting_exhibit_or_material",
        "cross_reference"
      ],
      "sourceTextExcerpt": "2. Actuarial opinion and supporting actuarial memoranda requirements are provided in VM-\n30 for companies that file the life, A&H annual statement or the fraternal annual statement . \nCompanies that file the property/casualty (P/C) annual statement or the health annual \nstatement will follow the actuarial opinion and supporting actuarial memoranda \nrequirements pursuant to the instructions for those annual statements. Such companies are \nnot subject to actuarial opinion and supporting actuarial memoranda requirem ents in this \nVM-30 unless the instructions for the P/C annual statement or the instructions for the health \nannual statement provide for requirements in VM -30. \nGuidance Note: It is the intent to allow the annual statement instructions to address all issues \nrelating to the actuarial opinion and memorandum for these two statements ( P/C annual statement \nand the health annual statement ), but not preclude the use of requirements as appropriate in VM -\n30 in the instructions for these two statements."
    },
    {
      "chunkId": "vm30-section-1-b-definitions-1-adverse-opinion",
      "sectionReference": "1. Adverse opinion",
      "pageStart": 326,
      "pageEnd": 326,
      "provisionTypes": [
        "definition_or_terminology",
        "exception_or_exemption"
      ],
      "sourceTextExcerpt": "1. The term “adverse opinion” means an actuarial opinion in which the appointed actuary \ndetermines that the reserves and liabilities are not adequate. (An adverse opinion does not \nmeet Section 3.A.7.e.)"
    },
    {
      "chunkId": "vm30-section-2-a-general-2-appointed-actuary-notice",
      "sectionReference": "2. Appointed-actuary notice within five business days",
      "pageStart": 326,
      "pageEnd": 326,
      "provisionTypes": [
        "scope_or_applicability",
        "appointed_actuary_responsibility",
        "commissioner_authority_or_process",
        "submission_or_timing_requirement",
        "retention_requirement",
        "exception_or_exemption"
      ],
      "sourceTextExcerpt": "2. Within five business days of the appointment of an appointed actuary, the company shall \nnotify the domiciliary commissioner of the name, title (and, in the case of a consulting \nactuary, the name of the firm) and manner of appointment or retention of each person \nappointed or retained by the company as an appointed actuary and shall state in the notice \nthat the person meets the r equirements of an appointed actuary. Once these notices are \nfurnished, no further notice is required with respect to this person unless the actuary ceases \nto be appointed or retained or ceases to meet the requirements of an appointed actuary ."
    },
    {
